  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =4(2x+{y}^{2}-z)\\,\\text{d}x+4y(2x+{y}^{2}-z)\\,\\text{d}y-2(2x+{y}^{2}-z)\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,2,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,2,0) & =4(2\\cdot 1+{2}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}x+(4\\cdot 2)(2\\cdot 1+{2}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}y-2(2\\cdot 1+{2}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =24\\,\\text{d}x+48\\,\\text{d}y-12\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,2+Δy,0+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈24\\,Δx+48\\,Δy-12\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1.5,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2-1=1, \\\\ Δy & =1.5-2=-0.5 \\\\ Δz & =1-0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈24\\,Δx+48\\,Δy-12\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈24\\cdot 1+48\\cdot (-0.5)-12\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ≈-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ decreases by approximately $-12.$",
